    code: 'AHI 401', title: 'Museum Studies: Provenance, Display & Repatriation', dept: 'AHI', level: 400, credits: 3, prereq: ['AHI 201'], format: 'seminar',
    blurb: 'How objects get into museums and how museums present them: provenance research, interpretation, access, ethics and the restitution debate, treated with sources on each side.',
    grading: GW, rubric: 'writing',
    portfolio: 'A provenance dossier on one object and a 300-word label written for general visitors.',
    resources: [SMART, MET_OA, { label: 'Creative Commons licences — overview', url: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/' }],
    units: [
      { id: 'u1', title: 'Provenance Research: Method and Evidence', weeks: 4, critique: 'seminar',
        teach: 'Provenance is the documented chain of ownership of an object. Build it backwards from the accession record: sale catalogues, dealer stock books, exhibition records, inventories and customs documents. Gaps are data, not failures; a dossier lists what it can prove, what it infers and what remains open. Pay close attention to 1933–45 and to colonial-era acquisition, where gaps may hide coercion or theft.',
        outcomes: ['Trace an ownership chain from published sources', 'Mark proven, probable and unknown links', 'Write a provenance statement in standard form'],
        studio: { prompt: 'Build a provenance dossier for one object in an open collection: a table of owners and dates, source for each link, and a 500-word narrative of the gaps.', deliverable: 'Provenance dossier (table + 500 words)' },
        study: [{ query: 'Egon Schiele drawing', sources: ['artic', 'met', 'smithsonian'], why: 'Check the record\'s provenance field. Which links are documented and which are only stated?', figure: true }, { query: 'Gustav Klimt', sources: ['artic', 'met', 'smithsonian'], why: 'What does the ownership history say about the object\'s journey through the 20th century?', figure: true }] },
      { id: 'u2', title: 'Display, Interpretation and Access', weeks: 4, critique: 'written',
        teach: 'A label is an argument. Ask who wrote it, for whom, and what the display leaves out. Good labels are short, concrete and lead with something to look at; the Chicago-style caption goes in the credit line, not in the text. Open access (CC0 images, data) changes what a museum can do for the public, but licences differ: check each collection and state your reuse terms. Accessibility matters too: height, contrast, alt text, audio description.',
        outcomes: ['Write a label for general visitors in under 100 words', 'Apply the right licence and credit for an image', 'Audit a display for access'],
        studio: { prompt: 'Curate a six-object online display from open-access collections around one question, with a 100-word wall label per object, a 200-word introduction, alt text, and a licence table.', deliverable: 'Online display (6 objects) with labels, intro, alt text and licence table', tool: 'LOREA' },
        study: [{ query: 'Japanese woodblock print', sources: ['met', 'artic', 'cleveland'], why: 'How would you label this so that a first-time visitor sees something before they read anything?' }] },
      { id: 'u3', title: 'Restitution and Repatriation: The Debates', weeks: 4, critique: 'seminar',
        teach: 'Claims for return arise from Nazi-era confiscation, colonial seizure, looting and unethical collecting of human remains. Museums, source communities, governments and scholars hold different positions: some argue for return on principle, others for shared stewardship or long-term loan, and some that universal museums serve broad access. Read the primary texts (the Washington Principles, NAGPRA, the Benin Bronzes agreements, the Parthenon dispute) and present each position in its own best terms.',
        outcomes: ['Summarise competing positions fairly with citations', 'Distinguish legal from ethical claims', 'Argue a reasoned view with acknowledged limits'],
        studio: { prompt: 'Write a 1,500-word briefing on one restitution case that sets out each party\'s claim with sources, the legal framework, and your own reasoned recommendation.', deliverable: 'Case briefing (1,500 words) + source list' },
        study: [{ query: 'Benin bronze', sources: ['met', 'smithsonian', 'europeana'], why: 'Read the credit line and provenance. What does it tell you, and what does it leave unsaid?', threeD: true }, { query: 'Parthenon sculpture marble', sources: ['met', 'europeana', 'smithsonian'], why: 'Which fragments are in which collections, and what does the object lose when divided?', threeD: true }] },
    ],
